    "result_text": "There is only one test class, and the full suite passes with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/E164.java`.\n\n**Change** \u2014 kept structural impact minimal:\n- Added one new, self-contained helper `nationalLengthValid(String)` that enforces `+61` \u2192 9 national digits and `+1` \u2192 10, leaving other country codes unconstrained.\n- Added a single term to the existing guard in `toE164` (`|| !nationalLengthValid(national)`) so a wrong length throws `IllegalArgumentException`, which the request pipeline surfaces as **400 Bad Request**.\n\nOnly one file was touched, the edited method (`toE164`) grew by one line, and the new logic lives in a small cohesive unit with trivial surrounding complexity \u2014 so the `WMC_other \u00d7 CC \u00d7 changed_lines \u00d7 files` cost stays low.\n\n**Why it's safe:** the valid `ownerNode()` telephone (`04XXXXXXXX`) normalizes to `+61` with exactly 9 national digits and still passes; seed data is inserted directly into the DB, bypassing `toE164`; and no test creates a `+1` owner through the API. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).",
